CloudBurrow patch 23 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1194, #1195). Applies after patch 0019.

The bug: CAST's FORMAT clause, and its AT TIME ZONE, were dropped when a
cast was turned into SQLite: the formatter wrote
googlesqlite_cast(expr, from, to, safe) and never read the ResolvedCast's
format and time_zone fields (go-googlesql already exposes them). So
CAST(TIMESTAMP '2020-01-01 05:00:00+00' AS STRING FORMAT 'YYYY') was
"2020-01-01 05:00:00+00", CAST(12.5 AS STRING FORMAT '999.99') was
"12.5", and CAST('2020' AS TIMESTAMP FORMAT 'YYYY') failed ("failed to
convert 2020 to time.Time type").

The patch:
- writes a cast with a FORMAT clause as googlesqlite_cast_format(expr,
  from, to, safe, format, zone), zone NULL when there is no AT TIME ZONE;
- adds internal/castformat, BigQuery's documented format elements
  (standard-sql/format-elements):
  - BYTES to and from STRING: HEX, BASE2, BASE8, BASE16, BASE32, BASE64,
    BASE64M, ASCII, UTF-8, UTF8;
  - DATE, DATETIME, TIME and TIMESTAMP to STRING: YYYY, YYY, YY, Y,
    Y,YYY, RRRR, RR, MM, MON, MONTH, DAY, DY, D, DD, DDD, HH, HH12, HH24,
    MI, SS, SSSSS, FF1-FF9, A.M., P.M., AM, PM, TZH, TZM, literals and
    "text", with case matching; a TIMESTAMP is written in the AT TIME
    ZONE zone, else UTC;
  - STRING to DATE, DATETIME, TIME and TIMESTAMP with the same elements
    and the format model rules (one of each part, DDD without a month,
    HH24 without a 12-hour element or meridian, a 12-hour element with a
    meridian, SSSSS alone, no element the target type lacks), missing
    parts defaulting to the current year and month, day 1 and midnight;
    a TIMESTAMP with no TZH is read in the AT TIME ZONE zone, else UTC;
  - INT64, FLOAT64, NUMERIC and BIGNUMERIC to STRING: 0, 9, X, x, ., D,
    ',', G, S, MI, PR, $, C, c, L, B, EEEE, FM, RN, V.
  Any other element, or a cast the FORMAT clause does not take, is an
  error (NULL with SAFE_CAST).

Tests: internal/castformat/castformat_test.go (the reference's
examples) and cast_format_test.go (through database/sql, literals and
columns).

